### 4B. Velocity Scoring (`scoring/velocity.py`)

Only applies to X/Twitter data. Uses the two-pass approach from Section 2.

```python
# 1. Retweet velocity (from two-pass re-check)
#    rt_velocity = (rt_count_t1 - rt_count_t0) / elapsed_minutes
#    Thresholds:
#      > 100 RT/min → velocity_score = 1.0
#      > 50 RT/min  → velocity_score = 0.8
#      > 20 RT/min  → velocity_score = 0.6
#      > 10 RT/min  → velocity_score = 0.4
#      < 10 RT/min  → velocity_score = 0.2

# 2. Cluster density (most important velocity signal)
#    Count how many tracked accounts post about the same topic within a 3-minute window.
#    Use fuzzy headline matching (Jaccard similarity > 0.6 on word tokens after removing stopwords).
#
#    cluster_size >= 4 accounts → velocity_score = max(velocity_score, 0.9)
#    cluster_size >= 3 accounts → velocity_score = max(velocity_score, 0.7)
#    cluster_size >= 2 accounts → velocity_score = max(velocity_score, 0.5)
#
#    Cross-source clusters are even stronger:
#    If cluster includes BOTH X tweets AND news API items → boost by 0.1
#    (This means the story is confirmed by wire services, not just Twitter chatter)

# 3. Quote-tweet ratio (secondary signal)
#    High quote-to-RT ratio = people adding commentary = higher significance
#    quotes / (retweets + 1) > 0.3 → boost velocity_score by 0.1
```

