## 11. Key Design Decisions

- **Syndication API first, not X's official API.** The free tier of X's API is write-only. The Syndication API is the public endpoint that Twitter's own embed widgets use — it returns ~20 recent tweets per profile, no auth needed. For 15 curated accounts polled every 2 minutes, this is more than sufficient.

- **News APIs are the primary content source, not X.** Finnhub and Polygon push the same breaking headlines that wire accounts on X post, usually within 1-2 minutes. They all source from the same wires (Dow Jones, Reuters, Bloomberg). X's unique value is the velocity/engagement layer on top — how fast people are reacting. Build the system so it works fully on news APIs alone, with X as enrichment.

- **Adaptive polling, not fixed intervals.** Polling every 60 seconds at 3 AM on a Sunday is wasteful. Polling every 60 seconds during FOMC day when VIX is spiking might be too slow. The scheduler should be market-hours aware and escalate automatically when activity spikes.

- **Two-pass velocity measurement.** Don't check RT counts at a fixed interval. Record engagement at first sight, then re-check 4 minutes later. The *rate of change* is the signal, not the absolute number.

- **Telegram over email for real-time.** Email has no push notification for most people. Telegram delivers in under a second with a phone buzz. Email is fine for the end-of-day digest.

- **SQLite, not Postgres.** Single-user tool. SQLite handles hundreds of items/day trivially. Zero config.

- **Keyword scoring first, ML later.** A well-tuned keyword scorer handles 90% of cases. Don't add embeddings or classifiers until the keyword approach demonstrably fails.

- **Dedup aggressively.** The same headline will hit 3-5 sources within minutes. Jaccard similarity > 0.6 on word tokens, within a 5-minute window. Keep the version with the most metadata as primary.

- **Respect rate limits religiously.** Track remaining calls per source. If exhausted, skip silently and try next cycle. Never crash on a 429.

- **Store everything.** Even items below trigger threshold. You need the history for backtesting scoring weights and for the daily digest.

- **Graceful degradation.** If X scraping breaks entirely (which it will periodically), the system should continue running on news APIs alone with zero manual intervention. Log a warning, not an exception.
